kadie@hal.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

> Let me play Devil's Advocate (in other words, I don't necessarily
> accept the argument I'm about to present):

So noted.

 
> The Supreme Court's definition of obscenity (the so-called _Miller_
> test) is:
> 
>    1) must appeal to the prurient interest of the average person
>    2) must describe sexual conduct in a way that is "patently offensive"
>       to community standards, and
>    3) when taken as a whole, it "must lack serious literary, artistic,
>       political, or scientific value"
> 
> I think the law behind "harmful to minors" laws is even less clear,
> but there is at least some case law that just says that access to
> material by minors can be outlawed if the _Miller_ definition
> *relative to minors* fits.

This actually sounds pretty reasonable, although as you said, with the lack
of actual case law on the subject, the potential for adverse precedent
being set is high given the country's mindset today.

 
> Because universities and libraries mostly only collect material of
> "serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value" it makes
> sense to exempt them from obscenity statutes.

But it doesn't, because all of the cases I've seen tried in concert with
the Miller decision have to satisfy all three criteria.  If universities
and libraries don't violate the third point of Miller, then there is no
need to exempt them to begin with.

 
> Adult book stores, on the other hand, pretty offer material *because*
> of its prurient value so it makes no sense to automatically exempt
> them from obscenity statutes.

But that's only one point of the Miller test.  If a store's contents do not
violate at least one point, then it doesn't qualify as 'obscene' and is
therefore exempt.

 
> Should universities and libraries really refuse exemptions in solitary
> with adult book stores? Would you really refuse an exemption for
> general, public-access computer services in solitary with adult book
> stores?

I think that falls under the category of "They should do it if they really
feel strongly about it".  Of course, no librarian in their right mind would
refuse a gratis exemption.  That's just being practical.  However, if a
bunch of libraries decided to stop being practical on this one point, I
would wholeheartedly support their stance.


> p.s. Please recall that I'm playing Devil's Advocate. I don't
> necessarily accept the argument I just presented.

No problem.  I've been reading your contributions to this newsgroup enough 
years to know what your views are.

