|> A few clarifications from the quotes of me (nothing serious):
|> 
|> I probably said this, but it is not exactly what I meant. Many sys
|> admin do stand up for their user's rights, so there is some history.
|> What is needed is a consensus that standing up for user's rights
|> is part of the job.

This system administrator does stand up for users rights and I do understand
that to be part of my job. There is an impression that those rights are
unlimited -- that is not the case. Users do not have the right to use computer
systems here at Western for anything they choose. Computer Systems are here for
specific academic purposes. That does not include MAKE MONEY FAST pyramid
schemes, slander, harassing e-mail, businesses, or the deliberate breaking of a
publication ban. Especially after 

1. being told that the computer system was not to be used for TTML
2. and after promising not to do so

The particular computer system is the property of an academic dept. and they
require that the system be used only for specific instructional purposes. It's
their machine they call the shots.

|>    Just as a professional librarian would have been less likely than
|>    Quinton (the university computer security officer) to turn over
|>    personal information to the police without a subpoena, so a
|>    professional reporter would have been less likely than Lt. Starbuck
|>    (the university student) to be so easily intimiated into disclosing
|>    sources to the authorities.

Hold on here. Librarians every day make choices about what they carry. They
usually don't carry Hustler magazine and that's never seen as censorship. 
Computer systems here have specific academic purposes. To restrict those
systems to that purpose is not censorship it's an allocation of scarce
resources. Free speach doesn't mean you can barge into a class room and
interrupt, or spray paint slogans on buildings, etc.

As for me turning over personal information to the police without a subpoena.
This is plain silly.
 
The policy around here is that users are responsible for what they do and we
have an obligation to be able to identify who those users are (eg. someone
broke into your machine from UWO, who was it?). If the police ask me do I know
Lt. Starbuck (they had his real name and address) who mailed this note I have
an obligation to say yes I know who he is. Having confirmed to the police that
the person they were looking is in fact a real live person using a system here
at UWO hardly counts as revealing personal information.  They already had his
full name and E-mail address. What is the deal?

All material provided to the police from the users account was with the full
cooperation of the user. If he really wished to press the issue he could have
held firm and taken a charge. He chose not too.

Finally, this isn't "deepthroat" and Watergate -- it's just a bunch of silly
fellows having fun at the considerable expense of others. If they really
believe in free speach why are they hiding behind pseudonym's? These anonymous
sources making outlandish statements is yellow journalism of the worse sort.  

