tittle@netcom.com (Cindy Tittle Moore) writes:

[...]
>While most US universities give general access to their students,
>most of them *do* have a "code of conduct" type of thing.

Yes, the student code of conduct at the U. of Illinois, for example, says:

 "A student at the University of Illinois at the Urbana-Champaign campus
 is a member of the University community of which all members have at
 least the rights and responsibilities common to all citizens, free from
 institutional censorship;"

and

 "A. Discussion and expression of all views is permitted within the
 University subject only to requirements for the maintenance of order."

and

 "The campus press and media are to be free of censorship. The editors
 and managers shall not be arbitrarily suspended because of student,
 faculty, administration, alumni, or community disapproval of editorial
 policy or content."

>So by getting a charter together for this group, we would simply gain
>the ability to complaint to a system administrator and say "this
>person is not posting accorting to the charter of the group."  It
>actually makes it much EASIER for the sysadmin when there is a clear
>charter for the group.
[...]

Clear or not, a sys admin can only enforce those parts of a charter
that are legal for him or her to enforce. For example, there used to
be a *.women.only newsgroup whose charter forbid males from posting.
The charter was very, very clear.

It is, however, illegal for all but a few U.S. universities to
discriminate on the basis of sex. The charter was clear, but it was
also unenforceable by universities.

Likewise, it is illegal for state universities in the U.S. to
discriminate on the basis of viewpoint or tone in public forums. And
while they can discriminate on the basis of topic, they must to so
fairly. They can't, for example, ignore posts against cruelty to
animals and punish posts for cruelty to animals.

- Carl
