>papresco@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca (Paul Prescod) writes:

>>It is common practice to disallow profanity in passwords.  Profane passwords
>>are much easier to crack then ordinary ones.  It is an unreasonable stretch
>>of the imagination to consider this "censorship on the information
>>highway."

>Your explanation doesn't wash. If their primary interest was in making
>passwords hard to crack, their banned word list would have more than
>just vulgarities.

The best passwords aren't even words.  The UofI passwords are required to
not even _be_ words.  Any combination of characters that matches up with
any name or word from the English dictionary is considered invalid.  People
trying to crack passwords always start with dictionary words.  They then
move the common ones to the front of the list.  Basically, if you don't use
your imagination in creating a password, then a hacker won't have to either.

Stuart Kahler


