In article <2s2itl$59q@news.bu.edu> kotta@acs4.bu.edu (Joseph Merlino) writes:
>--->	Interesting. So you're saying that the way you deal with infringements
>	on rights it to infringe them further?

No, I'm saying nothing of the sort.  First, I'm saying that
there is no absolute right to say anything you want in this
country.  Whether or not you agree that this is a necessary
or desirable thing is a different question.  Second, I'm
saying that the limitations that we do have on speech
generally center around how certain speech and/or expression
can limit the rights of others.  

Libertarian absolutism is deficient in a [large] number of
areas, but it particularly falls down around the question
of resolving rights in conflict.  That many and/or most of
the limitations on speech that we've been seeing these past
few decades don't actually protect any rights, civil or
otherwise, that we recognize in the Constitution (there is
no right not to be offended, as a prime example) does not
change the fact that certain forms of speech can impinge on
the legitimate rights of others.
