In article <tcmayCqFBr1.CyE@netcom.com> Timothy C. May, tcmay@netcom.com
writes:
>before. But the actions of certain rec.pets.cats zealots in attempting
>to silence the voices in this forum by getting accounts yanked--for
>the *views expressed*, not for wholesale spamming or bombing!--is
>"censorship" by the broad meanings in all dictionaries. Saying that
>only governments can censor misses the point completely.)

Let's reconstruct the crime, shall we?

a)Person A posts a message which many others find offensive. This is not
a violation of rights, nor is it illegal. (Good lord, if being offensive
were illegal, I'd be in prison.)
b)Person B, offended by the message, responds -- but not to Person A.
Person B responds to the owner of the system from which Person A posts.
Call this "Person C". This, too, is free speech. If I don't like what
someone has to say, I have the perfect right to express the fact I don't
like it to whomever I wish.  To deny me the right to do so, would be,
well, censorship -- no?
c)Person C reads Person A's complaint. Normally, he would ignore this
sort of thing, because it happens all the time. But when it is followed
by complaints from Persons D through Z, he takes notice. He is charged,
among other things, with preserving the reputation of his service, and he
knows that, on the net, reputation is everything.
d)Person C rules that Person A is abusing the system and should be
temporarily silenced, as a warning.

Near as I can tell, the entire train of events is a sequence of free
speech and reaction to that speech, all stages both legal and moral. The
"outrage" and cries of "censorship" are coming only from those who didn't
get quite the reaction they WANTED from their speech. They thought they
had the game rigged, but someone beat them at it, and they got their
wrists slapped. Now, they parade up and down the net screaming "Unfair!"
and "Down with the Imperialist Cat Owners!" and, of course, "We'll get
you NEXT time!"

As a general note, I came into this whole affair after the deed had been
done. I read rec.pets.cats to find reassurance that my own brain-dead
fluffies are more-or-less normal. However, I found the screams of
"censorship" to be disturbing, since it creates a "boy who cried wolf"
phenomenon, so I felt obliged to butt in.

Of course, I *enjoy* butting in.
