Chris@treehome.demon.co.uk (Chris Bourne) writes:

[...]
>   odd that the US Constitution is unable to distinguish between the right of 
>   an individual to publish any views, no matter how obnoxious, and the right 
>   of a group of individuals to publish their own views in one place, without 
>   preventing other individuals from publishing their contrary views in a 
>   different place.
[...]

The U.S. Constitution (as interpreted by the courts) is actually very
good at protecting the "right of a group of individuals to publish
their own views" free on contrary views. For example, if you have
editorial control of a newspaper, no law can compel you to publish
something you don't want to publish. In other words, in the U.S. there
is no right of reply (except on our censored airwaves) [see refs].

But, just as the government won't compel you to publish contrary views
in forums you over which you have editorial control, neither will it
compel people with contrary views to stay out of public forums.

In terms of Usenet,
   The government cannot require "equal time" in moderated newsgroups.
   The government cannot practice viewpoint discrimination in 
        unmoderated newsgroups.

- Carl

