I agree that Mr. Quinton makes his case well, but would like to offer
some cautionary comments.

Reg Quinton (reggers@julian.uwo.ca) wrote:

:Users do not have the right to use computer
:systems here at Western for ...
:or the deliberate breaking of a
:publication ban.

It has never been established in a court of law that a USENET publication
is "publishing".  For some press controls, the Criminal Code takes a
*VERY* broad definition of publishing.  For most others, a very narrow
one: it must be a periodical publishing at least every 31 days, it must
either charge a price or have advertising, etc.  The publication ban is
based in no one criminal code section(!) but on Judge Kovac's belief,
based upon case precedents, that courts have an "inherent jurisdiction"
over press coverage of their cases.  So which definition of "publishing"
should be used is simply not clear in the Code!

A court does have the power under law to decide that a USENET post
is publishing.  The police do not.  The Attorney General does not.
Mr. Quinton does not.  In Mr. Quinton's place, I like to think that
I would feel my responsibility to my clients, and to the Charter of
Rights, would require me to take the narrowest possible defintion of
publishing and tell the police that I am unable to accept their 
unsubstatiated word upon the subject and ask for court confirmation.


: The particular computer system is the property of an academic dept. and they
: require that the system be used only for specific instructional purposes.

A good point that makes the whole previous post moot.  However, it can
be argued that simply using the computer itself to edit, copy, and 
distribute ANY material is a way to learn to use the machine and the
network.  And if a sysop does not cut off accounts EVERY time that he finds
students storing and copying Sherlock Holmes stories, EFF documents,
Coke machine contents in Berkeley, Claudia Schiffer pictures, etc, and
any other material not closely related to their courses, then he is
using this restriction as an excuse to arbitrarily censure some behaviours
and not others.  A particular activity is NOT
