Dear Mr Potter,

	Thank you for your letter of 11 May, 1994 in reply to my previous
letter concerning the removal of certain sex-related Internet newsgroups
at your institution.  I feel that you did not adequately answer my questions,
and am seeking further information from you and your staff.  Excerpts from
your letter are prefixed by ">".

> 	Thank you for your letter of 4 May, 1994 concerning the decision by
> AARNet to discontinue certain news feeds.  I suspect that you have sent
> your letter to the incorrect address since The University of Melbourne
> operates the AARNet hub and Internet link under contract from AARNet.
> I am, therefore, unable to pursue the matter of your complaint.

My letter made mention of a policy instituted by The University of
Melbourne's Registrar of Information Technology, Associate Professor Iain
Morrison, not a decision by AARNet.  This policy was mentioned in a news
posting by Robert Elz, who is a network administrator at your university.
Furthermore, Geoff Huston at AARNet has made it clear in the past that news
feeds are the responsibility of the individual AARNet members, not AARNet
itself.  Therefore, I believe that you and your staff are the appropriate
people to pursue the matter of my complaint.

If on the other hand, AARNet personnel did put pressure on your institution
to discontinue those news feeds, I would be interested in the names of those
personnel so that I can take up my complaint with them.

> 	It is probably important, however, that I point out to you that the
> misuse of communication facilities in the terms of the relevant Commonwealth
> acts is not something which any institution can sanction and it is,
> therefore, unlikely that any University will knowingly take feeds of the
> news groups you mention.

Could you please supply me with details of which Commonwealth acts apply to
the removal of the sex-related newsgroups, or refer me to the person who
provided you with any legal advice concerning these newsgroups?  I suspect
that you have only heard rumors of such acts.  It is important that proper
legal advice be obtained instead of giving into such rumors, especially for
a topic as sensitive as this one.

If the acts you allude to do indeed limit the distribution of sex-related
information over electronic communications media, then the academic freedom
of the staff members at your university could be at risk.  Researchers who
operate in areas related to human sexuality may be in violation of those
acts if they carry out an academic discussion electronically with their
collegues.  I believe it is therefore important that you clear up exactly
what the acts cover, and maybe consider petitioning the government to
change those acts before it is too late.

One point in my previous letter that you failed to address was why your
university's library contains so many references to matters related to
human sexuality, yet the same information cannot be distributed electronically
at your university.  As libraries become more electronic in nature in the
future, it is important that the acts which you allude to are clarified
and modified as necessary.  Otherwise, much of what currently resides in
university libraries could be banned under the Commonwealth electronic
communications acts, putting academic freedom at further risk in the future.

				Yours sincerely,



				Rhys Weatherley,
