I think that a half-decent way of determining whether someone can be
considered as bisexual would be to determine whether the said person
would actually accept a one-on-one relationship with someone from
either sex.  Of course, this can also be ambiguous.  If your friend
believes that he would not feel comfortable with another man (only)
then I think that he would be better described as heterosexual.  Note,
that this description is fairly arbitrary because how someone defines
their sexuality is very dependant on a myriad of social and personal
factors.

There might also be a distinction that could be made between actually
having sex and/or having a relationship with someone.  If one defines
sexuality as a purely physical attribute then one could argue that one
encounter of each type (same-sex and opposite-sex) would be sufficient
to classify someone as bisexual.  I personally think that it goes
beyond purely physical aspects and must depend on actual relationships
between people.  Unless he's trying to fool himself, I think he's
probably best placed to make the decision whether he is bisexual or
not.  From your description, I'd venture to say that he's not.

In terms of your experiences with women, you say "in the past" which
seems to imply that this is not presently occurring and most probably
that you're not considering the option of a relationship with a woman
in the future...  if that is so, then I think that defining yourself
as gay is probably accurate.

Does this make any sense at all?

