>From: rhys@fit.qut.edu.au (Rhys Weatherley)
>Was the decision based on resource shortages?  If so, why didn't the
>university target the newsgroups that its users don't read first, because
>they are the ones that are of the least use to the university's staff and
>students?

Because popularity does not necessarily reflect the relevance of a
given item to the university's mission.

Look at your university library as a comparison.  Do they gear their
book/magazine selection policies on raw popularity?  If this univer-
sity did that, magazines like _GQ_ and _Cosmopolitan_ would take pre-
cedence over _The ABA Journal_, _Communications of the ACM_, and si-
milar, more scholarly publications.  (I can state, with a high con-
fidence, that more people on this campus read _Cosmopolitan_ than 
read _Communications of the ACM_.)

Are you suggesting that the university should provide alt.fan.bart-simpson
to 200 readers instead of providing comp.sys.wooga to 20?  I can't neces-
sarily agree with that.

It's a two-headed sliding scale - popularity and relevance.  

I do agree that the decision should have been more public, but the
idea that popularity alone should decide the content of newsfeeds
is a bit too much.

I suspect that newsgroup selection processes should be based on a
psychometric chart.  With most psychometric charts, the relation-
ship is not one-to-one; therefore, one index is higher than the 
other.  For commercial or private sites, I suspect that the chart
would look like this (not to scale):

        Popularity
	   ___ 100
	    |
	    |
	    |
	    |		Newsgroup value
	    | 		       ___ 100
	    |			|
	    |			|                 Relevance
	    |                   |                    ___ 100
            |                   |                     |
            |                   |                     |
           --- 0                |                     |
                                |                     |
                                |                     |
                                |                     |
                                |                     |
                                |                     |
                               --- 0                  |
                                                      |
                                                      |
                                                      |
                                                     --- 0
                                        
so that popularity has more 'value' than relevance.  I suspect that 
most universities would reverse this charge, placing more emphasis 
on relevance.

Those who like abstract thinking may construct an extra-dimen-
sional pschometric chart (or additional charts) to cover network 
load and diskspace issues.  8)  Personally, I'd do it like this:

	- establish "value index" (composite of popularity and relevance)
	- establish "cost index"  (composite of avg # postings and avg size)
	- generate final choices by integrating these indices

Obviously, the high-cost/high-value newsgroups will take the lion's
share of resources - but the relevance issue should limit the values.

Neither relevance nor popularity is - or should be - the sole
determinant factor in newsgroup selection issues for most sites.       

--Wes

