>I think part of the issue here is how computing resources are viewed; 
>e.g., is the computer:
>
>  - a limited, specialized resource to be rationed and controlled, used 
>    by the few who specifically need it?
>or,
>  - an open communications tool like a telephone or paper mail, to be 
>    used by all members of the institution?
>
>Generally administrators tend to hold the former ("limited resources")
>view, while users tend to have the latter ("communications tool") view. 

Actually, many admins hold *both* views.  I manage several systems; any 
Engineering student/faculty/staff may be granted on-request access to the 
'flagship' system, but only selected persons (grad students, faculty, un-
dergraduates enrolled in particular courses or engaged in particular re-
search) may be granted access to the more powerful systems.  

The 'flagship' system carries as many net.resources as I can build;
currrently, it offers gopher, Usenet, email, and ftp, with WWW in
the works.  As a default, every user gets 5 Mbytes of diskspace; with 
justification, I can go as high as 10-15 Mbytes.  (Our PC labs offer
file-downloading-to-floppy services via FTP.)

The other systems do not offer as many net.resources; they simply
cannot stand the load created by Web surfers, gopher addicts, source-
code collectors, and the like.  These machines are very much "academic
use only."  I *have to* control those resources more closely; the guy
who wants 15 Mbytes of disk for his .zips/.wavs/etc is *not* as im-
portant as the person who needs that space for his research data.

Now, you tell me - do I have some obligation to offer the same 
facilities on every system?  I think not.  I believe that it's
important to offer such facilities *somewhere*, but there are (and
will always be) systems which are for "academic use only."

--Wes

